   "source": [
    "## Final Experimental Validation\n",
    "\n",
    "The final comparison uses five independent random seeds. For each seed, a new dense model is trained first; the static-60 and adaptive systems are then constructed from that same seed-specific dense model. This provides a paired experimental design.\n",
    "\n",
    "The notebook reports mean ± standard deviation across independent runs, bootstrap confidence intervals, paired Dense-vs-Adaptive and Static-60-vs-Adaptive tests, Wilcoxon signed-rank tests, Cohen's *d*, real instantiated Conv1D MAC counts, analytical energy estimates, measured hardware latency, hard-routing utilization, difficulty-versus-computation analysis, learned-vs-heuristic gating, an energy-objective ablation, rare-class robustness, and explainability.\n",
    "\n",
    "Claims of superiority should be made only where the corrected multi-seed results and statistical analysis support them. Computational reduction is measured from physically instantiated expert architectures rather than zero-valued channel masks. Energy remains an analytical proxy, while latency is measured directly on the execution hardware.\n",
    "\n",
    "The adaptive method should only be described as genuinely input-adaptive if the routing analysis shows use of multiple expert levels and a systematic relationship between estimated sample difficulty and executed computation. Cross-modality generalization is outside the scope of this PTB-XL notebook and should be presented as future work unless a second dataset is evaluated separately.\n"
   ]
